00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.000 position:98%
The thing that is the most interesting is the manipulation aspect and the interactive, individualized experience;

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:18.000 position:98%
it’s the ability to create an intuitive sense of the underlying mathematical concept.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:23.000 position:98%
I work at Natixis, in investment banking, in stock derivatives.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.000 position:72%
I am in charge of the scientific calculations.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:35.000 position:97%
I have a team of about twenty people who work on the integration of the pricers.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:48.000 position:97%
One group... integrates the pricers in a way so that they go into production without difficulty.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000 position:98%
And another group works on the calculation of sensibilities and the stress-tests.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000 position:92%
Derivatives, those are the small variations, a small delta,

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:03.000 position:97%
the stress-tests are large disturbances in a given market that we try to... well, that we simulate

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:16.000 position:97%
in order to see what will happen to a stock portfolio that we’ve managed if a major problem were to arise in the market.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.000 position:98%
I used it [Mathematica] one time for an internal program called BFI Campus;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.000 position:97%
it’s a sort of internal university with a certain number of training sessions.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000 position:83%
So, I offered to teach a session on local volatility.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:42.000 position:98%
It’s a concept that was “discovered,” so to speak, in the early 90s

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:48.000 position:78%
that does not necessarily come easily to everyone.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:02:00.000 position:98%
If we approach it via pure, traditional math, in the end we have a huge obstacle to overcome before we can get a sense of what it does.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:15.000 position:97%
The session that I proposed is “Local volatility without math.” It’s done using the Mathematica manipulator,

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:22.000 position:98%
the way that I demonstrate and use local volatility is via the Monte Carlo methods.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:35.000 position:92%
The Monte Carlo methods are extremely... calculation-heavy.

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000 position:73%
Let me say that there are plenty of advantages.

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000 position:98%
There’s the interactive graphing, the manipulators, and the controls;

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:53.000 position:80%
the ease of development, since it’s an interpreter;

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.000 position:67%
then, the speed, thanks to the compilation.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:08.000 position:98%
So, that’s a very valuable characteristic of Mathematica: to be able to compile like that and be able to execute the results.

